 ('ENT_LEV_ASHIMA','PER_ISR_IRON_AGE','Deity of the people of Hamath, named among the gods made by Samaria''s resettled peoples (2 Kings 17:30). Plausibly connected to a NW-Semitic goddess Asham/Ashima attested at Elephantine (Anat-Ashima) and possibly to the Aramaean cult of Hamath; later rabbinic tradition imagined her as a goat/bald-figure idol.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ADRAMMELECH','PER_ISR_IRON_AGE','God of Sepharvaim to whom child sacrifice (''passing through fire'') was made (2 Kings 17:31). Name = ''Adad/Adar is king'' or ''the king is exalted''; identity debated — linked variously to Adad, a solar/Molek-type cult, or an Aramaean/Assyrian deity. Paired with Anammelech.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ANAMMELECH','PER_ISR_IRON_AGE','God of Sepharvaim receiving child sacrifice alongside Adrammelech (2 Kings 17:31). Name = ''Anu/Anath is king''; proposed links to the Mesopotamian sky-god Anu, a lunar deity, or (via ''Anath-melek'') the Canaanite goddess Anat. Identity uncertain.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_NIBHAZ','PER_ISR_IRON_AGE','God of the Avvites, named with Tartak among Samaria''s resettled gods (2 Kings 17:31). Etymology obscure and possibly a scribal corruption; later Jewish tradition fancifully described a dog-shaped idol. No independent epigraphic attestation.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_TARTAK','PER_ISR_IRON_AGE','God of the Avvites, paired with Nibhaz (2 Kings 17:31). Etymology uncertain (possibly an Elamite/Iranian theonym or scribal corruption); rabbinic tradition imagined an ass-shaped idol. No independent attestation outside the biblical notice.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ASTRONOE','PER_PHO_IRON_AGE','Sidonian goddess known from Damascius (Life of Isidore): a mountain-mother/huntress who, on the youth Eshmun''s self-mutilation, revives and deifies him. Generally read as a hellenized aspect of Astarte tied to the Eshmun healing cult at Sidon.');
INSERT INTO entity_periods (entity_id,period_id,confidence,rationale,source_id,review_status)
INSERT INTO _r VALUES
 ('ENT_LEV_BAAL_ZEBUB','member_of','ENT_LEV_BIBLICAL_DEITIES','high','Baal-Zebub of Ekron is one of the biblically-attested minor Levantine gods grouped under the DDD hub collective.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ASHIMA','member_of','ENT_LEV_BIBLICAL_DEITIES','high','Ashima of Hamath is named among the foreign gods of the resettled Samaria peoples (2 Kings 17:30).'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ADRAMMELECH','member_of','ENT_LEV_BIBLICAL_DEITIES','high','Adrammelech of Sepharvaim belongs to the DDD set of biblically-named minor Levantine deities (2 Kings 17:31).'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ANAMMELECH','member_of','ENT_LEV_BIBLICAL_DEITIES','high','Anammelech of Sepharvaim belongs to the DDD set of biblically-named minor Levantine deities (2 Kings 17:31).'),
 ('ENT_LEV_NIBHAZ','member_of','ENT_LEV_BIBLICAL_DEITIES','high','Nibhaz, god of the Avvites, is among the foreign gods of resettled Samaria (2 Kings 17:31).'),
 ('ENT_LEV_TARTAK','member_of','ENT_LEV_BIBLICAL_DEITIES','high','Tartak, god of the Avvites, is among the foreign gods of resettled Samaria (2 Kings 17:31).'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ASTRONOE','member_of','ENT_LEV_BIBLICAL_DEITIES','medium','Astronoe is a minor Sidonian/Levantine goddess in the broader DDD-adjacent set; grouped with the cohort hub.'),
 ('ENT_CHR_BEELZEBUL','reception_of','ENT_LEV_BAAL_ZEBUB','high','The NT/Christian demon Beelzebub/Beelzebul is the polemical reception of the Philistine city-god Baal-Zebub of Ekron (2 Kings 1); the ''prince of demons'' derives from this local Baal cult/epithet. Homonym pair flagged. Object ENT_CHR_BEELZEBUL confirmed in DB (Christian).'),
 ('ENT_LEV_BAAL_ZEBUB','received_as','ENT_CHR_BEELZEBUL','high','The original Ekronite deity Baal-Zebub was later received as the Christian demon Beelzebub, completing the deity-to-demon transmission. Bidirectional pair kept per DB house style (cf. Eshmun<->Asclepius).'),
 ('ENT_LEV_BAAL_ZEBUB','cult_form_of','ENT_CAN_BAAL','medium','Baal-Zebub of Ekron is a local Philistine cult form of the wider Canaanite storm-god Baal Hadad (ENT_CAN_BAAL); ''Zebub'' likely garbles the Ugaritic Baal epithet ''zbl/Zebul'' (''Prince'').'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ASTRONOE','equated_with','ENT_CAN_ASTARTE','medium','Damascius'' Sidonian Astronoe is widely identified as a hellenized aspect of Astarte in her healing-cult role; ancient interpretatio equation. Object ENT_CAN_ASTARTE confirmed.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ASTRONOE','consort_of','ENT_PHO_ESHMUN','medium','In Damascius'' account Astronoe is the mother/lover who revives and deifies the Sidonian healer-god Eshmun, making her his consort in the local myth. Object ENT_PHO_ESHMUN confirmed.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ADRAMMELECH','sibling_of','ENT_LEV_ANAMMELECH','low','Adrammelech and Anammelech are the paired gods of Sepharvaim named together in the same child-sacrifice notice (2 Kings 17:31); paired/twinned figures.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_NIBHAZ','sibling_of','ENT_LEV_TARTAK','low','Nibhaz and Tartak are the paired gods of the Avvites named together in 2 Kings 17:31; paired/twinned figures.'),
 ('ENT_LEV_ANAMMELECH','equated_with','ENT_CAN_ANAT','low','One scholarly etymology parses Anammelech as ''Anath-melek'' (''Anath is king/queen''), tentatively linking the theonym to the Canaanite goddess Anat (ENT_CAN_ANAT); speculative interpretatio.');
